%% A linked program whose IMPORTED declaration names type parameters
%% (TGLP modules.tex, "Cross-module type checking"): "Where the imported
%% declaration names type parameters, as an exported one may, the call
%% instantiates them as a local call does: a parameter the importing module
%% holds open stays open across the module boundary and is fixed at the call,
%% the clauses of the called procedure being those of the linked program."
%%
%% `tie` is held open here --- imported and re-exported over the bare parameter
%% Y --- and `go/2` fixes it to Colour at the call.  The program is its own
%% root, so it names no root-scope type and the fixture turns on nothing but
%% the parameter list crossing the boundary.
%%
%% Until the linker carried a declaration's parameter list, the renamed
%% `worker:tie/2` and the entry-point alias `tie/2` reached the linked check
%% with no parameters, and Y was refused there as an undefined type --- while
%% worker.glp on its own was accepted, the list being parsed and seen.

Colour ::= red ; green ; blue.

imported procedure(Y) worker#tie(Y, Y?).

exported procedure(Y) tie(Y, Y?).
tie(A?, B) :- worker # tie(A, B?).

exported procedure go(Colour, Colour?).
go(C?, D) :- tie(C, D?).
